\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct with LoRA \citep{hu2022lora,dettmers2023qlora} on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat \citep{ding2023ultrachat} training split. Training uses 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB). Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}; the controlled comparison design is described in \S\ref{sec:comparison-design}. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours, GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}, and PPL.

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. Under TF evaluation, all fine-tuned variants converge: GAR-ours spans 0.49--0.51 ($\leq$2\,pp), PPL is 2.78 for all. IID and held-out protocols produce identical rankings (held-out GAR-ours differs by $\leq$1\,pp), ruling out data overlap as a confound. GAR-Dongre remains low (0.01--0.02) with no TF-AR gap, confirming that only concentrated last-token attention is vulnerable to distribution shift; scheduled sampling breaks this pattern (\S\ref{subsec:ss}).

Under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR-ours drops to 0.019--0.064, an 8--17$\times$ gap (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}a). The ordering---ThreadGate (0.064) $>$ LoRA-only (0.037) $>$ Uniform Gate (0.033) $>$ PCGrad (0.030) $>$ Base (0.019)---shows ThreadGate vs.\ LoRA-only is significant ($p$\,=\,0.004; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-stats}), yet the gap remains 7.8$\times$. A second seed confirms TF stability ($0.504\pm0.001$). We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-way comparison on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. TF evaluation uses IID UltraChat and 50 held-out conversations; AR evaluation uses $N$\,=\,50 held-out conversations (mean 3.6 user turns). All fine-tuned variants converge under TF ($\leq$2\,pp spread); the 8--17$\times$ TF-AR gap persists across all configurations. $\bar{g}\!\pm\!\sigma_g$: learned gate mean and std across 28 layers (LoRA-only has no gates). GAR-O = GAR-ours; GAR-D = GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}.}
\label{tab:four-way}
\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc cc c}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{IID (TF)}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Held-out (TF)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{AR ($N$\,=\,50)}} & \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7} \cmidrule(lr){8-9}
\textbf{Configuration} & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & 95\% CI & $\bar{g}\!\pm\!\sigma_g$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Baseline}} \\
Base              & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.23 & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.34 & 0.019 & {\scriptsize[.014,\,.024]} & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Gated variants}} \\
ThreadGate        & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & 0.50 & \textbf{0.02} & 2.78 & \textbf{0.064} & {\scriptsize[.041,\,.091]} & .93\,{\scriptsize$\pm$\,.05} \\
\;+\,PCGrad       & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.030 & {\scriptsize[.023,\,.038]} & --- \\
Uniform Gate      & 0.49 & 0.01 & 2.78 & \textbf{0.51} & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.033 & {\scriptsize[.025,\,.043]} & .96\,{\scriptsize$\pm$\,.05} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{10}{l}{\emph{Ungated}} \\
LoRA-only         & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.037 & {\scriptsize[.027,\,.050]} & 1.00 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under TF evaluation ($N$\,=\,45, $\pm$1\,std). Fine-tuned variants maintain GAR near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 (7--8\% decay T1$\to$T4); Base decays 80\%. AR GAR collapses to 0.019--0.064 for all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}).}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap intact. If the collapse is caused by attention misallocation rather than representation degradation, then directly correcting attention should recover GAR. We test this by adding a constant bias $\beta$ to pre-softmax attention logits for goal-token positions during generation:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{A}_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} = A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} + \beta \cdot \mathbf{1}[j \in \mathcal{G}],
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{A}$ denotes the modified logits before softmax. The additive form operates in log-probability space, providing uniform boost even when baseline attention is near zero (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}b). The response is sharply nonlinear: $\beta \in [0, 2]$ yields marginal improvement, then $\beta = 3$ triggers a phase transition to GAR\,=\,0.556 (9\% PPL cost). At $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 (PPL 1.77); the operating region $\beta \in [3, 5]$ exceeds the TF baseline while PPL remains below 1.8 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering}).

This closes the causal loop: if the collapse were due to representation drift in hidden states, no amount of attention reweighting could recover GAR. The recovery confirms attention misallocation---not representation degradation---as the proximal cause.

\subsection{Scheduled Sampling Compresses the TF--AR Gap}\label{subsec:ss}

If exposure bias is the root cause (\S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}), reducing the training-inference distribution mismatch should narrow the gap. Scheduled sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled}---mixing model-generated tokens into training prefixes with linearly increasing probability---directly targets this mismatch.

AR GAR-ours rises from 0.064 to 0.347 (seed 42; gap 7.8$\times$$\to$1.44$\times$) and 0.418 (seed 123; gap 1.20$\times$), yielding a two-seed mean of 0.383 (gap 1.32$\times$) with no PPL cost (1.44 vs.\ 1.43). GAR-Dongre also rises (0.019$\to$0.353; $N$\,=\,5 pilot)---the only intervention that moves both attention metrics. The benefit diminishes in longer dialogues (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), consistent with exposure bias compounding over turns.

